From bd17cb57ed4bb104cb296ff29e7c9d0772a85a3d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yaegerbomb42 <2.03348624e+08+yaegerbomb42@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 03:00:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(vm-image): install the PreToolUse hook and stop
 blanket-allowing risky tools
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The image pre-allowed Bash, Edit, Write and NotebookEdit in
/etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json and registered no hook, so a headless
`claude -p` run executed every tool unguarded. Now:

- pretooluse-hook.mjs (new): dependency-free hook client baked at
  /opt/swarm/hooks/pretooluse-hook.mjs (root-owned, 0555). It forwards the
  tool call to the app with the run's bearer token, blocks until the verdict
  arrives, and prints the Claude Code decision. Every failure path answers
  deny — an unreachable app, a bad token, unreadable JSON or a timeout — and
  it always exits 0, because a non-zero exit makes the CLI continue.
- managed-settings.json: registers the hook for every tool ("*", 330 s) and
  keeps only the read-only tools pre-allowed (Read, Glob, Grep, WebSearch,
  WebFetch, TodoWrite). Mutating tools now run only on the hook's allow.
- Dockerfile: bakes the hook read-only.
- READMEs: the vm-image README described the old (advisory) behaviour and
  claimed the risk gate could still deny a call after the fact; it now
  documents the synchronous contract, the hook env, the fail-closed rule,
  the older-image --settings fallback and the rebuild step. The app README
  documents the endpoint, the token semantics and the two new env vars
  (SWARM_VM_HOOK_BASE_URL, SWARM_FULL_AUTO).
---
