 ## Managed settings
 
 `managed-settings.json` is copied to `/etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json`
-(read-only, root-owned) and pre-allows the standard tools:
+(read-only, root-owned). It does two things:
+
+1. **Registers a `PreToolUse` hook** that runs
+   `/opt/swarm/hooks/pretooluse-hook.mjs` (also root-owned, `0555`) for every
+   tool call. The CLI spawns the hook *before* executing the tool and blocks
+   until it exits; the hook asks the app
+   (`POST /api/runtime/hooks/pretooluse`, bearer token from the run's exec
+   env) and prints the verdict. The app's risk classifier
+   (`lib/harness/risk.ts`) plus the user's decision therefore decide whether a
+   tool runs **before it runs** — an `rm -rf`, a `sudo` command, or a read of
+   `~/.ssh` never starts until it was allowed, and a denial means it never
+   starts at all.
+2. **Pre-allows only the read-only tools** (`Read`, `Glob`, `Grep`,
+   `WebSearch`, `WebFetch`, `TodoWrite`). Mutating tools (`Bash`, `Edit`,
+   `Write`, `NotebookEdit`) are deliberately *not* blanket-allowed: the hook's
+   `allow` decision is what lets them run. Pre-allowing them would execute the
+   tool without the hook ever being consulted on older CLI builds.
+
+Without the hook a headless `claude -p` run would either stop at the CLI's own
+permission prompt or — as it used to, when the settings blanket-allowed
+`Bash` — run every tool unguarded while the app only classified it from
+stdout after the fact. The hook is what makes the verdict synchronous.
+
+### Hook configuration
+
+The hook reads its endpoint from the run's exec environment (set by the
+harness, never argv):
+
+| Variable | Meaning |
+|----------|---------|
+| `SWARM_HOOK_URL` | Absolute URL of the app's `/api/runtime/hooks/pretooluse`. |
+| `SWARM_HOOK_TOKEN` | Per-run bearer token. It can only *ask*; it cannot approve. |
+| `SWARM_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` | How long the hook waits for a verdict (default `330000`, just above the app's 300 s approval TTL). |
+
+The command in `managed-settings.json` resolves the script as
+`${SWARM_HOOK_SCRIPT:-/opt/swarm/hooks/pretooluse-hook.mjs}`, falling back to
+`/workspace/.swarm/pretooluse-hook.mjs` — the copy the runtime seeds for VMs
+built from an older image (the same file, kept in sync by
+`lib/harness/hook-script.test.ts`).
+
+Every failure path answers `deny`: no URL/token, an unreachable app, a non-200
+response, unreadable JSON, or a timeout. A hook that exited non-zero without a
+decision would make the CLI *continue*, which is exactly the failure mode this
+design exists to prevent — so the script always exits 0 with an explicit
+decision on stdout.
+
+### Rebuilding
+
+The image must be rebuilt for the hook to take effect:
 
-```
-Bash, Read, Edit, Write, Glob, Grep, WebFetch, WebSearch, TodoWrite, NotebookEdit
+```bash
+npm run vm-image:build   # docker build -t swarm-vm:claude vm-image
 ```
 
-Without it a headless `claude -p` run stops at the CLI's own permission
-prompt and cannot do anything. These are the *CLI's* permissions only: the
-app's risk gate (`lib/harness/risk.ts`) still classifies every tool call the
-agent makes and can still ask the user or deny the call outright.
+An existing VM keeps the image it was created from. The runtime covers that:
+when a VM's managed settings have no `PreToolUse` hook it writes
+`/workspace/.swarm/run-settings.json` with the same hook and passes
+`--settings` to `claude` (`lib/runtime/hook-settings.ts`).
 
 ## Network
 
